# Postfix master.cf lines for the mail stamp (README.md beside this file).
#
# The shape is Postfix's own simple content filter (FILTER_README, "Simple
# content filter example"). Merge these into /etc/postfix/master.cf; do not
# replace the file with this one.
#
# 1. The filter service. pipe(8) runs stamp-filter.sh as the unprivileged
#    user noctornal-stamp, up to 10 at a time. null_sender= passes the empty
#    sender as empty, not as MAILER-DAEMON. Rq: a Return-Path header, and the
#    pipe quotes an address that needs it.
#
# ==========================================================================
# service type  private unpriv  chroot  wakeup  maxproc command + args
# ==========================================================================
noctornal-stamp unix  -   n       n       -       10      pipe
  flags=Rq user=noctornal-stamp null_sender=
  argv=/usr/local/libexec/noctornal-stamp/stamp-filter.sh -f ${sender} -- ${recipient}

# 2. Turn the filter on for mail arriving over SMTP ONLY, on the smtpd
#    service that faces the internet. Keep "...other stuff here..." as your
#    file has it and add the -o line under it.
#
#    Do NOT set content_filter in main.cf. The filter hands each message back
#    through sendmail, which goes through pickup; a main.cf content_filter
#    would send it through the filter again, for ever. Set on smtpd only, the
#    re-injected copy is delivered.
#
# smtp      inet  n       -       y       -       -       smtpd
#   -o content_filter=noctornal-stamp:dummy
#
# 3. The stamp attests the Authentication-Results header your milters write,
#    so the milters (smtpd_milters in main.cf) must run on the same smtpd.
#    They do: Postfix runs smtpd milters before the queue, and the content
#    filter after it.
